# Review routing by scope/type (docs/issues/0020, PRD.md R24: "CODEOWNERS
# routes reviews by scope/type"). Standard GitHub/GitLab CODEOWNERS syntax:
# later patterns win over earlier ones on overlapping paths, so the general
# org-tier rule sits first and the more specific type/config rules sit after
# it. This file is NOT a record (packages/core/src/discoverRecords.ts only
# discovers `.md` files under org/, products/<slug>/, teams/<slug>/) — it
# never enters the catalog and `lore validate` never inspects it; it only
# configures the git host's PR review requirement. See README.md
# "`.lore/` configuration" / "Review routing" for the full convention.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REPLACE EVERY `@your-org/...` BELOW BEFORE YOU USE THIS REPOSITORY.
#
# They are placeholders, and the failure mode is silent: a CODEOWNERS line
# naming a team your git host does not have routes review to NOBODY. The host
# does not error, `lore validate` never inspects this file (by design — it is
# host config, not a record), and the rule looks enforced while it isn't. The
# first thing you learn is that a promotion which should have required a
# reviewer didn't. See docs/issues/0108.
#
# If your organization is one team, that is fine — name one account on every
# line. One real account beats four aspirational groups.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Org-tier records (org/*, excluding org/type/ below) default to the
# architecture group — org-scope knowledge is cross-cutting by definition.
/org/**                        @your-org/architecture

# The type catalog (org/type/) and Lore's own deployment configuration
# (.lore/) are platform-level surfaces: changing what counts as a valid
# record type, or the identity/grant model itself, is a platform change
# regardless of which scope's contributor opened the PR.
/org/type/**                   @your-org/platform-maintainers
/.lore/**                      @your-org/platform-maintainers
/CODEOWNERS                    @your-org/platform-maintainers

# Product-tier records route to that product's own reviewers. One line per
# product as products/ grows; the line below matches the bundled
# products/acme/ example record, so delete it when you delete that example.
/products/acme/**              @your-org/product-acme-team

# Team-tier records route to that team's own reviewers. Add one line per team
# as teams/ grows, replacing <team-slug> with the real directory name.
# /teams/<team-slug>/**        @your-org/team-<team-slug>
